---
import "../styles/style.scss";

import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import NavBar from "../components/NavBar.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
---

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title="Lab in a Cube" description="" author="PIC1" />
  </head>

  <body class="home">
    <div class="scan-line"></div>

    <NavBar />

    <div class="frame">
      <div class="pixel-earth" aria-hidden="true">
        <!-- Orbit (ellipse) placed behind the earth -->
        <svg
          class="orbit-svg"
          viewBox="0 0 100 60"
          xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
          aria-hidden="true"
          focusable="false"
        >
          <ellipse
            cx="50"
            cy="30"
            rx="46"
            ry="18"
            fill="none"
            stroke="#000066"
            stroke-width="1"
            stroke-opacity="0.9"></ellipse>
        </svg>

        <div class="earth-wrap" style="background-image: url('/earth.png');">
        </div>
      </div>

      <article>
        <header class="center">
          <h1 transition:animate="slide">Lab in a Cube</h1>
        </header>

        <main transition:animate="slide">
          <p>
            Space is attracting much interest from young students in the wake of
            the Newspace Revolution, the democratization of access to space by
            small companies, universities, etc.
          </p>
          <p>
            This trend has exploded since the flight demonstration in 2003 of a
            1-liter satellite built by university students.
          </p>
          <p>
            Satellite launch costs have since dropped steadily, and a few
            thousand small satellites are already orbiting the Earth.
          </p>

          <hr class="separator" />

          <h1>Our Plans</h1>

          <p>
            To challenge young students to see the electronics inside
            PocketQubes.
          </p>
          <p>
            To build a DIY (Do It Yourself) PocketQube, where students can try
            out different combinations of sensors and their layouts, give
            commands via a computer as a ground station and see its effects.
          </p>
          <p>To make our PocketQube as cheap and user-friendly as possible.</p>
          <p>
            This DIY approach to satelite design and operation will be a
            powerful educational tool to teach students about satellite
            electronics, systems integration, observing the effects of design
            choices, all while learning through interactive, practical
            experience.
          </p>

          <hr class="separator" />

          <h1>Our Product</h1>

          <!-- TODO add alt -->
          <Image
            src="/diagrams/PIC1_sat_V2_1.png"
            alt={""}
            class="satellite-diagram"
            width="3456"
            height="1826"
          />

          <p>
            A 5x5x5 cm PocketQube satellite with a modular design and set-up
            instructions, designed for schools and universities to expose
            students to this engineering field, thus captivating the next
            generation of engineers, particularly interested in electronics or
            space.
          </p>

          <p>
            The satellite contains micro-controllers, sensors (magnetometer,
            gyroscope, camera), power generation via solar panels, and a
            communication system to send data to the improvised ground station.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also included is an instruction manual for the construction and
            operation of the satellite, as well as the ground station, to ensure
            that students can easily set up and use the system.
          </p>

          <br /><br />

          <h2>Our Mission</h2>

          <p>
            Emulate a PocketQube satellite in orbit mapping a city, as close as
            possible to how it's really done. This is important to enphasize, as
            this ensures a more engaging and educational experience, as students
            can see the real-world applications of the concepts they're learning
            about.
          </p>
          <p>
            Real satellites of this kind have magnetorquers to rotate around the
            altitude axis. To simulate this system, we can use a pair of
            Neodymium magnets, to simulate the magnetic field of the Earth, and
            inductor coils. This then allows us to control the satellite's
            rotation with the ESP32.
          </p>
          <p>
            With the rotation being possible, we then move to executing the
            mission. With a model of a city around the satellite, we use its
            camera to take photos as it rotates. Then, the ESP32 sends the
            photos to the Ground Station via the antenna.
          </p>
          <Image
            src="/diagrams/maquete.png"
            alt="Esquema do satélite com um mapa à sua volta, Debaixo do satélite situam-se dois ímanes. Na face frontal do cubo lê-se 'Painel Solar', na direita 'Câmera', em cima do mapa lê-se 'Magnetorque' e nos ímanes 'Neodímio'."
            class="satellite-diagram"
            width="850"
            height="540"
          />
        </main>

        <Footer />
      </article>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>
